Dear Hiring Team,

*Application for Electric Motor Design Engineer, Propulsion (Job 4013301009)*

I am writing to apply for the Electric Motor Design Engineer position on the Propulsion team. I am currently a Research Assistant at the *Power Electronics and Machines Centre (PEMC), University of Nottingham*, a leading research group for transport electrification and electric aircraft propulsion, having completed my *PhD in Electrical Engineering at Southeast University, Nanjing*, in September 2025. My work is the *design, multi-physics optimization, and dynamometer validation of high-speed, high-power-density PM machines*, and the hybrid-electric mission of the ES-30, with the X2 demonstrator's hybrid flight campaign ahead, is the kind of propulsion problem I would like to work on.

The most direct match to the role is hardware. Through a multi-year collaboration with *Inovance*, a world-leading EV traction-machine supplier, I took two oil-cooled *high-voltage PM traction machines* through the *full design-build-test cycle*: a *20,000 rpm, 270 kW* machine (380 Nm, *7.87 kW/kg*, 97.4% peak efficiency) and an 18,000 rpm, 113 kW machine. Each went from electromagnetic, thermal, and mechanical design through *engineering-drawing release and tolerance specification* to *AVL dynamometer characterization* across the full efficiency map. Power density and thermal margin governed every design decision, the same currencies that govern aircraft propulsion motors.

My design record also supports the role's *electric motor topology trade studies*. It covers interior PM, wound-field switched flux (magnet-free), and hybrid-excitation machines within a *multi-objective optimization* framework coupling electromagnetic FEA, lumped-parameter thermal networks, and mechanical-stress evaluation. I work in *JMAG, ANSYS Maxwell, and Motor-CAD* alongside an in-house Python finite-element code package, and at PEMC I am developing operator-learning surrogate models that cut evaluation time by more than 94% relative to direct FEA, which widens the design space a trade study can afford to cover.

On *design for manufacturing*, I developed a systematic _n_-layer flat-wire winding methodology for hairpin PM machines (_IEEE Trans. Transportation Electrification_), covering conductor layout and AC-loss reduction, and co-authored studies on stator-core manufacturing effects such as the vibration behavior of glued versus welded lamination stacks. The prototype programs gave me working knowledge of *stator winding, lamination stacking, and varnish impregnation* processes, and of rotor mechanical-stress design at 20,000 rpm, including stress-relieving groove design.

On *test-stand integration*, beyond the AVL dynamometer campaigns I designed, built, and commissioned a *three-phase full-bridge converter* and its control algorithm for machine prototype testing, and I model *PWM-excitation effects on loss and temperature* so that bench observations close the loop back into design.

I have authored 22 journal papers (17 in IEEE Transactions), 18 conference papers, and 9 patents, and received 1st Prize in the 2025 IEEE TEC Electric Vehicle Design Competition and the 2025 IEEE Transactions on Transportation Electrification Prize Paper Award. My resume is attached, and I would be glad to discuss how I could contribute to the ES-30 propulsion system.

Yours sincerely,
#v(0.2em)
